          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be flattened into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ho(C)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category
            %em derivator
            is a refinement of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ho(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            for many purposes, like computing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limits"} homotopy limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} homotopy categories
            of all categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            between them. Since derivators can be studied using only ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            theory, they are often practical in situations when one requires more information than the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category
            retains, but not the whole
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Very similar structures were invented independently by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            (who introduced the name “derivator”),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"} Alex Heller
            (who called his version “homotopy theories”), and Franke (who considered only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stable
            case and called his version a “system of triangulated diagram categories”). The definition given below combines elements from the work of all three.
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p The notion of derivator can be motivated in several ways.
          %h3#as_a_compromise_notion As a compromise notion
          %p
            Suppose we start from the perspective that what we really study in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category of an (∞,1)-category
            (the 1-category obtained by setting all equivalent 1-morphisms equal) is a fairly coarse invariant, but for some purposes it is sufficient. On the other hand, sometimes we need more than merely the homotopy category, but doing everything with
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
          %p
            A
            %em derivator
            is thus a compromise notion, containing more information than a homotopy category, but being easier to work with than an
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            theory. Thus derivators provide a “truncated” version of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            For instance, it turns out that we can also express many convenient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+properties"} universal properties
            in terms of derivators. A striking example is the theory of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(A)
            is not defined by a universal property. A natural statement would be that, given a triangulated category, the category of additive functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to T
            which send
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b(A)\to T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+category"} semi-simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} cone
            of a map in a triangulated category is not defined by a universal property. On the other hand, the cone of a morphism of complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            is canonically defined: this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
            of the diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ←
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\leftarrow X \to Y
          %h3#as_a_motivation_for_categories
            As a motivation for
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            On the other hand, we may ask: Why do we take for granted that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} spaces
            provides a good notion (among others) of
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            in higher groupoids anyway? A priori this may seem arbitrary (although it certainly works very well). Instead we might ask: what is the mathematical structure over which everything is canonically enriched? How can we even correctly formulate such a question?
          %p
            The notion of
            %em derivator
            provides a way to correctly formulate such a question, and the answer turns out to be mostly what we expect. Every type of “category theory” (at least, category theory without an
            %em a priori
            given
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enrichment
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#prederivators Prederivators
          %p
            Let
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            (not necessarily even
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            be some 2-category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"}> diagram
                s
            %em all
            small categories (which generates
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %strong prederivator
            with domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            is a strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Dia
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia^{op} \overset{D}{\to} Cat
          %p
            As usual,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{op}
            denotes the 1-cell dual of a 2-category. Thus, a prederivator is a “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            \-valued
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PDer
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PDer
            whose morphisms are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformations"} pseudonatural transformations
            and whose 2-cells are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modification"} modification
          %p
            Another common convention is to use the double dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Dia
                    %mi coop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia^{coop}
            which reverses both 1-cells and 2-cells, although confusingly sometimes in the literature this double dual is still denoted “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Dia
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia^{op}
            ”. The motivation for the latter choice seems to be that then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(A)
            is the category of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            with values in
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            of shape
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Dia
                    %mi coop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia^{coop}
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Dia
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia^{op}
            via the 2-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{op}
            (as long as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            is closed under opposite categories in
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %p
            There are two main motivating examples. Firstly, any category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            defines a “representable” prederivator by the assignation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\mapsto Hom(A,C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in Dia
            to the category of functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PDer
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PDer
          %p
            Secondly, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            which sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in Dia
            to the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)(A)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,C)
            relative to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} objectwise weak equivalences
            (as we allowed categories in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            to have large
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} (2-)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %h3#derivators Derivators
          %p
            A
            %strong derivator
            is a prederivator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            which satisfies a list of axioms. These axioms are of two sorts.
          %p
            The first set of axioms says that there exist well-behaved homotopy limits and colimits, and more generally
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong (Der3)
                For any functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u : X\to Y
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Dia
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^*:D(Y)\to D(X)
                admits a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_!
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi u
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_*
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mo !
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mover
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi u
                                %mo *
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi u
                                %mo *
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi u
                              %mo !
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      (u_! \dashv u^* \dashv u_*)
                      \:
                      D(X)
                      \\stackrel{
                      \\overset{u_!}{\to}
                      }
                      {
                      \\stackrel{
                      \\overset{u^*}{\leftarrow}
                      }{
                      \\underset{u_*}{\to}
                      }
                      }
                      D(Y)
            %li
              %p
                %strong (Der4)
                For any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+square"} comma square
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mi g
                            %mtd
                              %mi B
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⇙
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mi v
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi C
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mi u
                            %mtd
                              %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{A &amp; \overset{g}{\to} &amp; B \\
                      ^f\downarrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \downarrow^v\\
                      C&amp; \underset{u}{\to} &amp; E}
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Dia
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+transformation"} Beck-Chevalley transformation
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mo !
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mtext and
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %msup
                        %mi v
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! g^* \to u^* v_! \quad \text{and} \quad v^* u_* \to g_* f^*
              %p
                are
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Kan+extension#Pointwise"} pointwise
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
                (for the first case) and when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is so (for the second).
          %p
            The second set of axioms are “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"}> sheaf
            ” conditions. Of course, we cannot assert that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is exactly a sheaf (in the appropriate
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} 2-categorical sense
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            is 2-categorically dense and so any sheaf on it is representable (and represented by
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} covering family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{Y_i \to X\}
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Desc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X) \to Desc(D,\{Y_i\})
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
            to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            data for the covering, while not an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketch"} sketch
            conditions.
          %p The standard axioms are:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong (Der1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi Dia
                        %mi coop
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\colon Dia^{coop} \to Cat
                takes
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(\emptyset) = 1
            %li
              %p
                %strong (Der2)
                For any
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Dia
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in Dia
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon 1\to X
                determined by the objects of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mi x
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X) \overset{(x^*)}{\to} \prod_x D(1)
              %p
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative+functor"} conservative
                (though not generally faithful). This in turn implies that the same is true for any jointly
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
                family of functors.
            %li
              %p
                %strong (Der5)
                For any
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Dia
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in Dia
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                denotes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X\times I) \to Hom(I,D(X))
              %p
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full
                (though again, it is not generally faithful). Since this functor is also conservative by (Der2), it is then a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+smothering+functor"} weakly smothering functor
          %p
            There is substantial variation in (Der5). Sometimes it is convenient to assume this property when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is any (perhaps finite)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+category"} free category
            %strong strong
            if it satisfies (Der5).
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: It’s clear to me that these are desirable requirements, which are moreover satisfied by all derivators of the form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
              = succeed "." do
                %em sufficient
          %p
            It is easy to see that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has pointwise left and right Kan extensions along all functors in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            (or more generally, has well-behaved homotopy Kan extensions), then the prederivator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            is also a derivator. Thus the derivator encodes the notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
            and of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
          %h3#SemiDerivators Semiderivators
          %p
            It may sometimes be useful to consider prederivators which are like derivators, but which “do not have
            %em all
            limits and colimits.” Let us say that a
            %strong semiderivator
            is a prederivator satisfying (Der1), (Der2), and (Der5).
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a semiderivator,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in D(B)
            is a
            = succeed "-shaped" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\colon B\to C
            is a functor in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            %strong pointwise left extension
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            is an object “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_! x
            ” in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to v^* v_! x
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x / v^*)
            (this says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_! x
            is a “local” or “partial” value of the left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_!
            at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_!
            may not exist) which additionally satisfies the
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition#Local"} local Beck-Chevalley condition
            for any comma square as above. We have a dual notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong pointwise right extension
          %p
            We say a semiderivator is
            %strong complete
            (resp.
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong cocomplete
          %h3#historical_remarks Historical remarks
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Grothendieck’s definition of a
                %em derivator
                included only axioms (Der1), (Der2), (Der3), and (Der4).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"}> Alex Heller
                ‘s definition of a
                %em homotopy theory
                included axioms (Der1), (Der2), (Der3), a weaker form of (Der4), and (Der5) for finite free categories. His
                %em pointed homotopy theories
                add the axiom of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed derivator
                %em stable homotopy theories
                include a weaker version of the axiom now used for a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stable derivator
            %li
              %p
                Franke’s definition of a
                %em system of triangulated diagram categories
                was irreducibly
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
                to consist of categories enriched over pointed sets. (See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed derivator
                for the relationship of this approach to that of adding a “pointedness” axiom to an unpointed notion of derivator.) In this context, he assumed axioms analogous to (Der1), (Der2), (Der3), (Der4), and (Der5) for the interval category, plus the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stability
                axiom. He also observed that if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
                consists entirely of posets, then (Der4) follows from the other axioms.
          %p
            Axioms (Der1)–(Der4) are clearly the easiest to motivate and the most obviously necessary. Axiom (Der5) is used in order to do things with morphisms in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(*)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(I)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
          %h2#computing_homotopy_kan_extensions Computing homotopy Kan extensions
          %p
            We now describe an “omnibus” theorem which is the main thing enabling us to compute with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
          %h3#equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            \-equivalences
          %p
            For any functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon I\to J
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %strong equivalence
            if the induced transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi J
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi J
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\pi_I)_! (\pi_I)^* \to (\pi_J)_!(\pi_J)^*
            is an isomorphism, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_I\colon I\to *
            is the projection to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_J
            %em constant
            diagrams of shapes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            are equivalent. By the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi J
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi J
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(*)((\pi_J)_!(\pi_J)^* X , Y) \to D(*)((\pi_I)_!(\pi_I)^* X , Y)
          %p
            is an isomorphism for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y\in D(*)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi J
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi J
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi I
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(J)((\pi_J)^* X , (\pi_J)^*Y) \to D(I)((\pi_I)^* X , (\pi_I)^*Y)
          %p
            is an isomorphism—i.e. that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^*
            is fully faithful when restricted to the image of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi J
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_J^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^*
            is fully faithful, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is a
            = succeed "-equivalence." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            In a representable derivator (i.e. in ordinary category theory), the colimit of a constant diagram of shape
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} copower
            with the set of connected components of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "-equivalence," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
          %p
            By contrast, in the derivator coming from a model category or
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is a copower with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            regarded as an
            = succeed "-groupoid," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-equivalence." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          #NerveEquiv.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"}> Alex Heller
                , Cisinski)
              A functor which induces a homotopy equivalence of nerves is a
              = succeed "-equivalence" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            This was proven by
            %a{:href => "#Heller"} Heller
            using the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+derivator"} enrichment
            of any derivator over
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basic+localizer"} basic localizer
            = succeed "-equivalences" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Any functor in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
              with a fully faithful left or right adjoint is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\dashv g
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^* \dashv f^*
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is fully faithful, then the unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to g f
              is an isomorphism, and thus so is the unit
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\to f^* g^*
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^*
              is also fully faithful and so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_D
              denote the class of
              = succeed "-equivalences" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_D
              is saturated, in the sense that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon I\to J
              is a morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dia
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
              which becomes an isomorphism in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msubsup
                        %mi W
                        %mi D
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia[W_D^{-1}]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Fix some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y\in D(*)
              and consider the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Dia
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi\colon Dia \to Set^{op}
              which sends
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mi I
                        %mo *
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mi I
                        %mo *
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(I)(\pi_I^*X, \pi_I^*Y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
              inverts
              = succeed "-equivalences," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msubsup
                        %mi W
                        %mi D
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia[W_D^{-1}]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              becomes an isomorphism in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Dia
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msubsup
                        %mi W
                        %mi D
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia[W_D^{-1}]
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
              = succeed "-equivalence" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              vary).
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p
              For any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "-equivalences" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basic+localizer"} basic localizer
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Saturation gives 2-out-of-3 property and closure under retracts. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              has a terminal object, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to 1
              has a fully faithful right adjoint and hence is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi u
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo
                              %mi v
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mi w
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ A &amp; &amp; \overset{u}{\to} &amp; &amp; B\\ &amp; _v \searrow &amp; &amp; \swarrow_w \\ &amp; &amp; C, }
            %p
              to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_! \pi_A^* \to w_! \pi_B^*
              is an isomorphism. By (Der2) it suffices to check this for any
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi v
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi v
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mtext and
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi w
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi h
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi w
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{v/c &amp; \overset{g}{\to} &amp; A\\
                    ^f\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^v\\
                    *&amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; C} \quad\text{and}\quad
                    \\array{w/c &amp; \overset{k}{\to} &amp; B\\
                    ^h\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^w\\
                    * &amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; C}
            %p
              and the transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^* v_! \pi_A^* \to c^* w_! \pi_B^*
              factors as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi c
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi w
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi c
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mo !
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^* v_! \pi_A^* \cong f_! g^* \pi_A^* \cong f_! \pi_{v/c}^* \to h_! \pi_{w/c}^* \cong c^* w_! \pi_B^*
            %p
              using the analogous map for the functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v/c \to w/c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v/c \to w/c
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            Therefore, since the nerve equivalences are the smallest basic localizer, every nerve equivalence is a
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %h3#ExactSquares Exact squares
          %p Now suppose given any square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi L
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ⇓
                            %mi μ
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi u
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi J
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{L &amp; \overset{p}{\to} &amp; I\\
                  ^q\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow^\mu &amp; \downarrow^u\\
                  J &amp; \underset{v}{\to} &amp; K}
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dia
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dia
            which commutes up to a specified 2-cell
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %strong exact
            if the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+transformation"} Beck-Chevalley transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_! p^* \to v^* u_! \quad \text{and} \quad u^* v_* \to p_* q^*
          %p
            are isomorphisms. (In fact, if one of these is an isomorphism, so is the other, since they are
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mate"} mates
          %p
            Like the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+square"} exact square
            in ordinary category theory, this is a “functional” definition, but we can also give a more explicit characterization, using more or less the same argument. Given such a square as above and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j)
            for the category whose objects are triples
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi i
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi α
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big(\ell\in L,\, i\overset{\alpha}{\to} p(\ell),\, q(\ell)\overset{\beta}{\to} j\big).
          %p
            The morphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j)
            are morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma\colon \ell \to \ell'
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            which make the evident triangles commute. Now there is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\colon (i/L/j) \to K(u(i),v(j))
            (the latter considered as a discrete category), which sends the above triple to the composite
          #eq:commacomp.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi μ
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u(i) \overset{u(\alpha)}{\to} u(p(\ell)) \overset{\mu}{\to} v(q(\ell)) \overset{v(\beta)}{\to} v(j)
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              A square as above is
              = succeed "-exact" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in J
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j) \to K(u(i),v(j))
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p It is easy to verify that the horizontal or vertical pasting composite of exact squares is exact; hence if the given square is exact, then so is the composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi p
                          %mtd
                            %mi I
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi q
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi u
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi J
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi v
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{(L/j) &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; L &amp; \overset{p}{\to} &amp; I \\
                    \\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^q &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^u\\
                    * &amp; \underset{j}{\to} &amp; J &amp; \underset{v}{\to} &amp; K}
            %p
              for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in J
            #eq:ijsquare.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi u
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi i
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi v
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{(i/L/j) &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; *\\
                    ^g\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{u(i)}}\\
                    * &amp; \underset{v(j)}{\to} &amp; K}
            %p are exact. But the square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi u
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ,
                            %mi v
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi s
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi u
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi i
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi v
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{K(u(i),v(j)) &amp; \overset{s}{\to} &amp; *\\
                    ^t\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{u(i)}}\\
                    * &amp; \underset{v(j)}{\to} &amp; K}
            %p
              is exact, since it is a comma square, and by the universal property of a comma square, the square
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ijsquare"} (2)
              factors uniquely through this one by a functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\colon (i/L/j) \to K(u(i),v(j))
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              defined above. Specifically, we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi s
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = s r
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = t r
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_! f^* \to (v j)^* (u i)^*
              is equal to the composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mo !
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi s
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi s
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_! f^* = t_! r_! r^* s^* \to t_! s^* \to (v j)^* (u i)^*
            %p
              where the second map is the Beck-Chevalley transformation for the comma square, which is an isomorphism. Thus,
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ijsquare"} (2)
              is exact just when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mo !
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi s
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi s
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_! r_! r^* s^* \to t_! s^*
              is an isomorphism. But this says exactly that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is a
              = succeed "-equivalence." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            A square is said to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+exact+square"} homotopy exact
            if it is
            = succeed "-exact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (or, equivalently, for all
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              A square is homotopy exact if and only if for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in J
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j) \to K(u(i),v(j))
              induces a weak homotopy equivalence of nerves.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              “If” follows directly from Theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NerveEquiv"}
              and the previous theorem. Conversely, we can take
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              to be the derivator of spaces (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-groupoids), where the
              = succeed "-equivalences" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p
              For each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in J
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi\colon u(i) \to v(j)
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j)_\varphi
              denote the subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j)
              consisting of those triples for which the composite
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:commacomp"} (1)
              is equal to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i/L/j)_\varphi
              has a contractible nerve.
          %p
            This gives a convenient way to compute many homotopy limits and colimits, which works in any derivator, and
            %em a fortiori
            in any
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+exact+square"} homotopy exact square
          %h2#2CategoryOfDerivators The 2-category of derivators
          %p
            We can consider now the 2-category of derivators. Actually, there are several different ways to define such a 2-category, depending on whether we require morphisms to preserve homotopy colimits, limits, both, or neither. Let us write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Der
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Der_!
            for the 2-category whose morphisms preserve colimits. Thus:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                its
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %li
              %p
                its 1-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
                s are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformations"} pseudonatural transformations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:D\to D'
                which commute with the functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_!
                (i.e. the canonical comparison maps for these are isomorphisms),
            %li
              %p
                its 2-cells are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
                between these.
          %p
            We write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Der
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_!(D,D') = Der_!(D,D')
            for hom-categories in this 2-category.
          %h3#free_cocompletions Free cocompletions
          %p
            Now, given a small category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-functor," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Der
                    %mo !
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi D
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Der_!\to Cat \quad , \qquad D\mapsto D(X).
          %p
            Note that, for any (pre)derivator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
            is canonically equivalent to the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(X,D)
            of morphisms of
            %em prederivators
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (considering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as a representable prederivator). Of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            will usually not itself be a derivator, but nevertheless this
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %em representable
            in the 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Der
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Der_!
          %p
            Thus, there exists a derivator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{X}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:X\to \widehat{X}
            (called the Yoneda embedding), such that, for any derivator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ^
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_!(\widehat{X},D)\simeq Hom(X,D)=D(X).
          %p
            In other words, the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:X\to \widehat{X}
            is the “free completion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by homotopy colimits” in the sense of derivators.
          %p
            Furthermore,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{X}
            can be described rather explicitly: it is the derivator associated to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model category of simplicial presheaves
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} homotopy theory of simplicial sets
            gives rise to the derivator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo *
                      %mo ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{*}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            stands for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            category. Note that in order to conclude this, we didn’t take for granted that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
          %p
            From there, you can see that any derivator is canonically enriched in the derivator
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo *
                      %mo ^
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi SSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{*}\simeq Ho(SSet)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            acts uniquely on any prederivator,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo *
                    %mo ^
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi SSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{*}\simeq Ho(SSet)
            acts uniquely on any derivator (as far as we ask compatibility with homotopy colimits). Thus the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            might be reformulated vaguely as: is there an algebraic model of
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo *
                      %mo ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{*}
          %h2#constructions_of_derivators Constructions of derivators
          %p
            Viewing a derivator as a partway point between an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
            and in particular
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
          %h3#from_homotopy_theory From homotopy theory
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W)
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_C(X) = Cat(X,C) = C^X
            comes equipped with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^X
            on each category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^X
            %em homotopy prederivator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            by inverting these weak equivalences in each diagram category:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)(X) = Ho(C^X,W^X) = C^X[(W^X)^{-1}]
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_dwyer_hirschhorn_kan_and_smith Theorem (Dwyer, Hirschhorn, Kan, and Smith)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
              with a functorial
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  three-arrow calculus
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/three-arrow+calculus"} ?
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
              is a prederivator satisfying axioms (Der1), (Der2), and (Der5).
          %p
            Actually, it is enough to assume that, for every small category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^X
            admits a (not necessarily functorial) three-arrow calculus, but in practice this only happens when we have a functorial three-arrow calculus for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_cisinski_2003 Theorem (Cisinski, 2003)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              (complete and cocomplete, but possibly without functorial factorisations), then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
              is a derivator (with the strong version of Der5).
          %p
            Axioms (Der1) is easy to check, and when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has functorial factorisations, we have a functorial three-arrow calculus. Axiom (Der3) is also easy, since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limits"} homotopy limits
            and colimits in categories with weak equivalences are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            we really are studying the things we want to study.) Axiom (Der4) requires a bit of work; there is a proof for combinatorial model categories using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure"} injective model structure
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Groth"} (Groth)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RadBan06"} (Radulescu Banu)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RadBan06"} (Radulescu Banu)
          %p
            Note that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is any complete and cocomplete 1-category, we can equip it with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+model+structure"} trivial model structure
            in which the only weak equivalences are isomorphisms. Then the above derivator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            is the same as the representable prederivator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_C(X)
          %p
            In this connection, it is interesting to point out that a given homotopy category can admit multiple “enrichments” to a derivator. For instance, the homotopy category of the model category of chain complexes over a field is equivalent to the category of graded modules over that field, which is itself complete and cocomplete. Thus we have two distinct derivators, which have equivalent underlying homotopy categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(*)
          %h3#FromInfinityCategories
            From
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            obtained by identifying equivalent
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphisms"} 1-morphisms
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(-)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_1
          %p
            Since categories are in particular
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} functor (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C^X)
            %em homotopy prederivator
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)(X) = Ho(C^X).
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} limits
            and colimits in the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            is a derivator (this is an interpretation of the theory of pointwise Kan extensions in
            = succeed "-categories)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GPS14"} GPS, Example 2.5
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and of the derivator associated to the Dwyer-Kan localization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is discussed by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Lenz"} Lenz
          %h3#exotic_examples Exotic examples
          %p
            There are also examples of derivators not satisfying (Der5) that do not arise from homotopy theory or higher category theory in this way. For instance, see Remark 5.4 and Example 5.5 in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LN16"} Lagkas-Nikolos
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#PresentableDerivatorsAndCombinatorialModelCategories Presentable derivators and combinatorial model categories
          %p
            We discuss precise versions of the idea that derivators indeed constitute a model of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
          #RenaudinTheorem.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              The sub-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"}> locally presentable
              ” derivators with left adjoints as morphisms between them, among the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              of all derivators (see
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#2CategoryOfDerivators"} above
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalent
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-localization"} 2-localization
              of the 2-category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+proper+model+category"} left proper
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+categories"} combinatorial model categories
              at the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalences"} Quillen equivalences
          %p
            This is shown in (
            %a{:href => "#Renaudin06"}> Renaudin 2006
            ). See at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28CombModCat%29"} Ho(CombModCat)
            for more.
          %p
            Notice that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} locally presentable (∞,1)-categories
            are precisely those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            that arise, up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localizations"} simplicial localizations
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+categories"} combinatorial model categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalence of 2-categories
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of presentable derivators and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-category"} homotopy 2-category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"} (∞,2)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pr%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} Pr(∞,1)Cat
          %p
            As a corollary, we obtain a canonical comparison
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi PrDer
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Pr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ho(PrDer)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  Ho(Pr(\infty,1)Cat)
          %p
            from the homotopy category presentable derivators with left adjoint morphisms, to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category of an (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pr%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} Pr(∞,1)Cat
          %p
            This is induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-localization+of+a+2-category"} 2-localization
            from the fact that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^H
            that forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localizations"} simplicial localizations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi CombModCat
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Pr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L^H \;\colon\; CombModCat \longrightarrow Ho(Pr(\infty,1)Cat)
          %p
            sends
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalences"} Quillen equivalences
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
            (see thereresentable infinity-category#PresentedByCombinatorialSimplicialModelCategories)).
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %p Special kinds of derivators:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed derivator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stable derivator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+derivator"} monoidal derivator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+derivator"} enriched derivator
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord
                finitely complete prederivator
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/finitely+complete+prederivator"} ?
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord
                regular derivator
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/regular+derivator"} ?
          %p The calculus of homotopy Kan extensions used in derivators:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+exact+square"} homotopy exact square
          %p Special limits and structures in derivators:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+in+a+derivator"} pullback in a derivator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism+in+a+derivator"} monomorphism in a derivator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend+in+a+derivator"} coend in a derivator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system+in+a+derivator"} orthogonal factorization system in a derivator
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The term
            %em derivator
            is originally due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
            \. The first fifteen chapters of a 2000 page manuscript of Grothendieck (in French) about derivators can be found at:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/groth/Derivateurs.html"} Les Dérivateurs
          %p
            Independently, there is a version due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"} Alex Heller
            (who called them “homotopy theories”):
          %ul#Heller
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"}> Alex Heller
              ,
              %em Homotopy theories
              , Memoirs of the American Mathematical Society, Vol. 71, No 383 (1988).
          %ul
            %li
              Alex Heller,
              %em Stable homotopy theories and stabilization
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1431157"} MR
              – see
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+derivator"} pointed derivator
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stable derivator
          %p Apparently also independent is the development by Franke, who takes an enriched approach to the pointed case and also assumes stability:
          %ul
            %li
              Jens Franke,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Uniqueness theorems for certain triangulated categories with an Adams spectral sequence
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0139/"} K-theory archive
          %p Georges Maltsiniotis has written an introduction to the topic (in French):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
              %em Introduction à la théorie des dérivateurs, d’après Grothendieck
              , Preprint (2001)
              %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/ps/m.ps"} ps
          %p
            He also gave a
            %a{:href => "http://congreso.us.es/htag09/php/index.php?carga=courses"} course
            (in English) in Seville, Sep 2010, and part 3 is on derivators:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"} Georges Maltsiniotis
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a{:href => "http:webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Lecture_I_Localizers.pdf"} Lecture I (Localizers)
                  %p
                    %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Lecture_II_Model_Categories.pdf"} Lecture II (Model Categories)
                  %p
                    %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Lecture_III_Derivators.pdf"} Lecture III (Derivators)
                  %p
                    %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Summary_on_Derivators.pdf"} Summary on Derivators
          %p Part of the above material is adapted from
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/03/a_perspective_on_higher_catego.html#c032227"} blog comment on derivators
          %p
            Cisinski has also written a number of papers on the subject (in French), which can be found at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.uni-regensburg.de/cisinski/publikationen.html"} his homepage
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                — Images directes cohomologiques dans les catégories de modèles.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Ann. Math. Blaise Pascal
            %li
              %p
                — Catégories dérivables, Bull. Soc. Math. France, Tome 138 (2010) no. 3, pp. 317-393.
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/BSMF_2010__138_3_317_0/"} NUMDAM
          %p
            Derivators were also recently used by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"} Gonçalo Tabuada
            in a universal characterization of higher
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gon%C3%A7alo+Tabuada"}> Gonçalo Tabuada
              , “Higher K-theory via universal invariants”
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.2420"} arXiv
          %p An introduction to some of the theory of pointed and stable derivators, in English, can be found in the paper:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Denis-Charles Cisinski
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Amnon+Neeman"} Amnon Neeman
              %em Additivity for derivator K-theory
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2382732"} MR
          %p
            An introductory discussion aimed towards
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stable derivator
          %ul
            %li#Groth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moritz+Groth"}> Moritz Groth
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Monoidal derivators and additive derivators
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1203.5071"} arxiv/1203.5071
              %em Derivators, pointed derivators, and stable derivators
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-bonn.de/~mgroth/groth_derivators.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p Other references include:
          %ul
            %li#RadBan06
              %p
                Andrei Radulescu Banu, Cofibrations in Homotopy Theory,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610009"} arXiv:math/0610009
            %li#Lenz
              %p
                Tobias Lenz, Homotopy (Pre-)Derivators of Cofibration Categories and Quasi-Categories, Algebr. Geom. Topol. 18 No. 6 (2018), pp. 3601–3646.
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.07845"} arXiv:1712.07845
            %li#IN16
              %p
                Ioannis Lagkas-Nikolos,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Levelwise modules over separable monads on stable derivators
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.06340"} arXiv:1608.06340
            %li#Coley20
              %p
                Coley, Ian,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The theory of half derivators
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12057"} arxiv:2010.12057
            %li#Richardson20
              %p
                Richardson, James,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Enriched derivators
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.07740"} arXiv:2010.07740
            %li#GPS14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moritz+Groth"}> Moritz Groth
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kate+Ponto"} Kate Ponto
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Mayer-Vietoris sequences in stable derivators
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1306.2072"} arxiv:1306.2072
          %h3#relation_to_categories
            Relation to
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            An brief informal discussion of derivators as a 2-categorical tool for studying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              \-categories
            is contained in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              %em Squeezing Higher Categories out of Lower Categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/05/squeezing_higher_categories_ou.html"}> blog
              )
          %p In the paper
          %ul
            %li#Renaudin06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivier+Renaudin"}> Olivier Renaudin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Plongement de certaines théories homotopiques de Quillen dans les dérivateurs
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0603339"}> arXiv:math/0603339
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2009.02.014"} doi:10.1016/j.jpaa.2009.02.014
          %p
            it is proven that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"}> locally presentable
            ” derivators is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            of the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+categories"} combinatorial model categories
            at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalences"} Quillen equivalences
            (see also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28CombModCat%29"} Ho(CombModCat)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable (∞,1)-category
          %p Along somewhat similar lines, in
          %ul
            %li
              Kevin Arlin,
              %em On the ∞-categorical Whitehead theorem and the embedding of quasicategories in prederivators
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.06980"}> arxiv:1612.06980
              )
          %p
            it is shown that
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em strict morphisms
            between the latter (which is arguably somewhat against the spirit of derivators, but still interesting), and that at the 2-categorical level the embedding is conservative but generally fails to be full on large
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ul
            %li
              Daniel Fuentes-Keuthan, Magdalena Kedziorek, Martina Rovelli,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A model structure on prederivators for (∞,1)-categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.06496"} arxiv
          %p
            On lifting derivators from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} homotopy categories of (infinity,1)-categories
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-categories"} homotopy 2-categories
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C2%29-categories"} (infinity,2)-categories
          %ul
            %li#DiVittorio23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicola+Di+Vittorio"}> Nicola Di Vittorio
              ,
              %em 2-derivators
              (2023). &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05216"}> arXiv:2309.05216
              \&amp;rbrack;
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  The condition of left properness does not appear in the arXiv version of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Renaudin06"} Renaudin 2006
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dugger%27s+theorem"} Dugger's theorem
                  (see
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "combinatorial+model+category#EveryCombinatorialModelCatQEquivalentToLeftProper"} here
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zag"} zig-zag
                  of Quillen equivalences between left proper combinatorial model categories may be taken to pass through only left proper ones.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
        .revisedby
